DEVICE DISCOVERY AND READ-ONLY BASELINE

Before touching a device, identify real attached hardware, pin
ownership, controller mode, and the current measured state.

SAFE INITIAL COMMANDS
  pins
  ls /dev
  ls /dev/gpio
  ls /dev/adc
  ls /dev/i2c
  pwmctl status
  fbctl status
  help pwmctl
  help i2cctl

INTERPRETATION
  pins is a finite live pin map: free, input, output, framebuffer,
  UART, I2C, ADC or PWM as appropriate. It is not a wiring diagram.
  GPIO numbers are SoC pin indices, not physical header positions.
  An assigned pin may already be driven by a background program.
  GP0..15 are used by the teaching board's LED matrix, GP16/17
  input switches, GP18 buzzer, GP19 servo motor, GP20/21 UART1, and GP26/27 I2C1
  on this teaching setup. GP25 is the LED only on standard Pico;
  Pico W uses wireless-chip LED control instead.

DO NOT
  - Run 'cat /dev/gpio/N' without a bound: a live digital device
    may supply 0/1 indefinitely.
  - Assume a read from a live GPIO node supports lseek.
  - Start interactive gpio/fbed/mem/watch from MCP.
  - Write to a physical output merely to test whether it works.

BEFORE STATE CHANGE
  Confirm which device and pin, electrical limits, user intent,
  expected result and rollback. If any of these are unknown, ask
  or restrict actions to read-only inspection.
